`merge_sort_links.hpp`: the sort shared by `list` and `intrusive_list`. It works on the links
only, merging as a binary counter: runs of 1, 2, 4, ... nodes wait in 64 slots and each node is
merged while still in cache (the scheme of libstdc++'s `list::sort`), instead of log n sweeps over
the whole list. `merge_link_chains` takes the left run first among equals, so the sort is stable,
and the back links are rebuilt in one final pass. No
element is moved, copied or compared more than O(n log n) times, no memory is allocated, and an
element's address stays the same, so pointers into the list survive sorting. `link_run` holds the
two ends of a chain while it is relinked.
